(3. Vulnerabilities Identified and Remediated) Tj
0 -14 Td
0 -14 Td
(V1 – Weak Session Cookie Security: The original application relied on default PHP session) Tj
0 -14 Td
(handling without HttpOnly, Secure, or SameSite flags, allowing theft or reuse of session) Tj
0 -14 Td
(identifiers. We centralised session bootstrap logic in inc/security.php to set hardened) Tj
0 -14 Td
(cookie parameters and regenerate IDs after login.) Tj
0 -14 Td
0 -14 Td
(V2 – Absence of CSRF Protection: Login, registration, and booking forms lacked anti-CSRF) Tj
0 -14 Td
(checks, enabling forged requests. Each form now embeds a per-session token generated by) Tj
0 -14 Td
(generateCsrfToken\(\) and validates it server-side before mutating state.) Tj
0 -14 Td
0 -14 Td
(V3 – SQL Injection in Authentication Flows: Raw string interpolation was used when looking) Tj
0 -14 Td
(up users, making login bypass trivial. All database access has been refactored to use) Tj
0 -14 Td
(parameterised mysqli prepared statements across admin/index.php, login.php, register.php,) Tj
0 -14 Td
(and carDetails.php.) Tj
0 -14 Td
0 -14 Td
(V4 – Insecure Password Storage: User and admin passwords were stored as legacy MD5 hashes.) Tj
0 -14 Td
(Registration now stores passwords using password_hash\(\), and the login flows transparently) Tj
0 -14 Td
(upgrade any remaining MD5 hashes to bcrypt on successful authentication.) Tj
